//? Title: Native plotting across language bindings
//? Subtitle: Original noisy Doppler data and the same canonical B-spline model in sixteen languages
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Retain the original sixteen complete language programs: the same noisy Doppler
target,1000 generated sample positions and a canonical B-spline fit, rendered
through the native plotting API. An introductory complete C++ program makes the
data-fit-predict-plot path visible. Each program owns its original seed and
executes independently; the pictures are language-specific executions, not
asserted byte-identical random samples or fits across ecosystems.
)

\toc

> Data, model and native graphics

For each program the target is sin(20/(x+0.25)) on[0,1], with additive
standard-normal noise. The100 argument to BSR selects100 equally spaced cubic
basis functions, as in the original. This is regression, not a classification
benchmark or a comparison of language performance. Synthetic data is the
appropriate controlled fixture; no external dataset is introduced.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/plot_pipeline.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    dm::set_seed(42);
    const auto x = dm::runif(0, 1, 1000);
    const auto y = dm::sin(20 / (x + .25)) + dm::rnorm(0, 1, 1000);
    const dm::BSR fit(x, y, 100);
    const auto grid = dm::seql(0, 1, 1000);
    const auto prediction = fit.predict(grid);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("data", {0, 0}, std::format("Actual generated rows\n{}", x.size()));
    d.node("fit", {8, 0},
           std::format("Actual native BSR fit\n{} basis functions, degree {}",
                       fit.basis_size(), fit.degree()));
    d.node("prediction", {16, 0},
           std::format("Actual prediction rows\n{}", prediction.size()));
    d.edge("data", "fit").edge("fit", "prediction");
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    d.save_svg("build/plot_pipeline.svg");
    std::print("| actual training positions | responses | prediction grid | predictions |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               x.size(), y.size(), grid.size(), prediction.size());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual training positions | responses | prediction grid | predictions |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1000 | 1000 | 1000 | 1000 |
\image(figures/plot_pipeline.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual native generated-row and prediction sizes around the canonical B-spline fit.)
\alttext(Three native diagram nodes show1000 actual generated rows, the actual native BSR basis count and degree, and1000 actual predictions.)

> Sixteen complete original language programs

> Plot Example

A simple plotting example: noisy samples of the Doppler function, the true mean,
and a B-spline fit -- the same program in C++ and in each language binding: C#,
D, Go, Guile, Java, JavaScript, Lua, OCaml, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, R, Ruby
and Tcl.

Each block runs in the nix shell `.#report-<language>`, which provides the
language and its nix-built datamunge binding, so nothing needs building first
(the first run of a language builds its binding, later runs reuse it). Each
program saves its figure under `build/`, and the block shows it.

\tabs(
\tab(C++,
```{cpp, nix-shell="git+file:../../../..#report-cpp", cmd="c++ -std=c++23 -O2 {file} -o {bin} $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge) && {bin}", file="figures/plot.svg"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    dm::set_seed(42);
    const auto doppler = [](const dm::Vector<> & x) {
        return dm::sin(20 / (x + 0.25));
    };
    const auto x = dm::runif(0, 1, 1000);
    const auto y = doppler(x) + dm::rnorm(0, 1, 1000);
    const auto xseq = dm::seql(0, 1, 1000);
    const auto yseq = doppler(xseq);
    const dm::BSR bsr(x, y, 100);
    const auto yfit = bsr.predict(xseq);
    const auto plt =
        dm::plot().title("Doppler data").points(x, y, "Samples", "#edc78799").lines(xseq, yseq, "True Mean", "#c80a2c", 3.).lines(xseq, yfit, "Estimated Mean", "#2c0ac8", 3.).x_label("X").y_label("doppler(X)").size(1000, 600);
    dm::mkdir("build");
    plt.save("build/plot.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/plot.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual native plot from the C++ program:1000 noisy samples, the true Doppler mean and the fitted cubic B-spline curve.)
\alttext(Actual native plot from the C++ program:1000 noisy samples, the true Doppler mean and the fitted cubic B-spline curve.)
)
\tab(Python,
```{python, nix-shell="git+file:../../../..#report-python", results="markdown"}
import os
import numpy as np
from pydatamunge import datamunge as dm


def doppler(x):
    return np.sin(20 / (np.asarray(x) + 0.25))


dm.set_seed(42)
x = np.array(dm.runif(0, 1, 1000))
y = doppler(x) + np.array(dm.rnorm(0, 1, 1000))
xseq = np.array(dm.seql(0, 1, 1000))
yseq = doppler(xseq)
yfit = dm.BSplineRegression(x, y, 100).predict(xseq)
os.makedirs("build", exist_ok=True)
(
    dm.RPlot.create().title("Doppler data").points(
        x, y, "Samples", dm.RGB(237, 199, 135, 0.6), 4.0
    ).lines(xseq, yseq, "True Mean", dm.RGB(200, 10, 44), 3.0).lines(
        xseq, yfit, "Estimated Mean", dm.RGB(44, 10, 200), 3.0
    ).xlab("X").ylab("doppler(X)").save("build/plot_python.svg")
)
# Keep and reference the native renderer's saved SVG.
import shutil
os.makedirs("figures", exist_ok=True)
shutil.copyfile("build/plot_python.svg", "figures/plot_python.svg")
print(r"\image(figures/plot_python.svg)")
```
